\define spans-its-signature(a:"one"
b:"""a triple-quoted default
""" c:'a single-quoted default
' d:[[a bracketed default
]])
A macro body carrying $a$, $b$, $c$ and $d$.
\end
\procedure spans-its-variable-default(e:((a variable default
)))
A procedure body carrying <<e>>.
\end
\parameters (size:"22pt"
colour:"red")
A pragma signature opens on one line and closes on another. Every default quoting `\define` itself
reads stands above, in its own signature, because the grammar carries one region per quoting and a
reader sits inside whichever it opened.

A VARIABLE DEFAULT READS ONLY IN A `\procedure`, `\function` OR `\widget`. `\define`'s own parameter
list excludes a bare `)`, so a `((...))` default there would stop the signature at its FIRST closing
paren — folding the rest of the line, the `\end` after it, even a pragma standing below, into one
ordinary paragraph. The variable default above stands in a `\procedure` signature instead, where the
host's own parameter grammar keeps a nested `))` together on purpose.
